export const lsConcepts501600: any[] = [
  { id: "ls-nama-501", title: "Guḍānnaprītamānasā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is fond of sweet rice made with raw sugar. The compound joins guḍa, unrefined cane sweetness, with anna, cooked rice, and a mind delighted by it, describing Lākinī, the yoginī of the ten-petalled navel centre. Her taste for this humble festive dish shows the Goddess meeting devotees through everyday nourishment rather than grandeur, turning the kitchen into an altar. As guardian of the flesh layer, she converts digested food into strength for practice, so offering and eating become acts of shared vitality. Contemplate each meal as prasadam from the navel lotus, eating slowly with gratitude so bodily energy settles into steadiness for meditation.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-103"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-502", title: "Samastabhaktasukhadā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who confers happiness on all Her devotees. The word joins samasta, all without exception, with bhakta, the loving worshipper, and sukha, ease and joy given freely, again as Lākinī acting for Lalitā. Her role is not distant awe but intimate welfare: every sincere devotee of the Mother receives fitting delight, whether outer relief or inner calm. This assures seekers that fierce yoginī forms are mothers first, distributing happiness according to each heart rather than rank or learning. When anxious about worthiness, recall this promise and rest in devotion. Contemplate her as the smile behind all blessings, and end daily practice by mentally sharing any peace received with all beings.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-103"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-503", title: "Lākinyambāsvarūpiṇī", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is in the form of the lākinī yoginī. The name fuses Lākinī with ambā, mother, declaring this yoginī not a subordinate spirit but the Mother herself wearing a particular functional form. She closes the maṇipūra group of names, gathering the preceding details of colour, food, tissue and attendants into one maternal person. Doctrine here teaches that cosmic powers are not fragments of the Goddess but her complete presence focussed at one bodily station. Hence worship of the centre reaches the whole. Contemplate the navel lotus saying inwardly, mother Lākinī, uniting reverence for the part with devotion to Lalitā as the undivided all.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-103"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-504", title: "Svādhiṣṭhānāmbujagatā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who resides in the six-petaled lotus in the svādhiṣṭāna, kākinī yoginī. Sva means own, adhiṣṭhāna means seat, so this is the centre of one's own dwelling, located above the root in the pelvic region. Its six petals carry six consonants with bindus, its pericarp showing an eight-petalled lotus and crescent moon holding the water seed vaṁ, linked in practice with the fire seed for awakening subtle capacities. Prosperity and fluidity belong here because water governs flow, feeling and generativity. Contemplate a soft crescent moon resting on still water at the lower belly, letting breath move like tides while dedicating emotional energy to clarity rather than craving.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-104"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-505", title: "Caturvaktramanoharā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who has four beautiful faces. Catur means four, vaktra means face, manoharā means captivating, portraying Kākinī gazing in every direction with gracious beauty. Her fourfold face marks this as the fourth station in this hymn's ascending count and mirrors completeness of outlook, while teachings on radiant currents link the psychic centres with measured cosmic circulation from root to brow. Beauty here signals harmony of the waters: when feeling is purified, perception becomes luminous in all quarters. Contemplate her four faces as mindfulness of body, feeling, thought and awareness together, turning attention gently whichever way distraction pulls, and returning beauty to the mind.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-104"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-506", title: "Śūlādyāyudhasampannā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who possesses the trident and other weapons (i.e., noose, skull and abhaya). Śūla opens the list, the trident that pierces the triple knots of habit, followed by noose that draws the wandering mind home, skull that reminds of impermanence, and hook that steers the elephant of desire. Variant scriptural lists name conch, discus, club and lotus, showing living traditions describe her armoury differently without changing her protective office. Weapons in her hands are surgical tools, not aggression. Contemplate one inner weapon at a time, for example the noose gently retrieving a distracted thought, and feel guardianship replacing self-reproach during meditation.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-104"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-507", title: "Pītavarṇā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is yellow in color. Pīta names the golden-yellow hue of Kākinī, warm like ripened grain, turmeric and early sunlight. Colour in this system is not decoration but diagnosis: yellow marks the maturing of vitality at the water centre, earth beginning to refine emotion into discernment. Her golden glow suggests confidence without harshness, energy made kindly and digestible for the seeker. Visual fierceness elsewhere is here softened into ripeness. Contemplate a mild golden light suffusing the lower belly, as though sunlight warming a field, and let nervous agitation settle into a steady, friendly alertness that supports practice.  Golden ripeness further suggests that sustained practice slowly sweetens temperament, replacing green reactivity with mellow discernment others can trust.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-104"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-508", title: "Atigarvitā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is very proud. Ati means exceedingly, garvitā means proud or self-exulting, ascribing to Kākinī a regal delight in her own beauty and power. The commentary contrasts this with Lalitā herself, who as Nirmadā is utterly free of pride, since one who is and owns everything has nothing left to boast about. The yoginī's pride thus mirrors the seeker's own vanity when a little beauty, talent or attainment arrives. It warns that partial plenitude intoxicates while wholeness humbles. Contemplate any swell of self-importance after good meditation, silently offering it upward with the thought, this beauty is hers, not mine, until confidence becomes gratitude.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-104"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-509", title: "Medoniṣṭhā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who resides in the fat in living beings. Medas means the fatty tissue, niṣṭhā means firmly stationed, placing Kākinī as guardian of this fourth bodily layer beneath the skin. Counting flesh, blood and succeeding tissues upward, the hymn aligns each yoginī with one layer, so physiology itself becomes a rosary of her residences. Fat here signifies stored reserve, insulation and unhurried sustenance rather than mere excess. Honouring her in this tissue sanctifies embodiment instead of despising it. Contemplate the body's reserves as her granary held in trust, resolving to nourish, exercise and rest the body moderately so stored strength can serve steady spiritual effort.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-105"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-510", title: "Madhuprītā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is fond of honey and other offerings made with honey. Madhu spans honey, sweetness, springtime and the savoury liquor of cooked offerings, and Kākinī delights in them all. Sacred fire rites blend ghee with honey and milk in small measure, and the Vedic hymns praise honeyed oblations, so her taste roots personal devotion in ancient sacrificial hospitality. Sweetness offered outwardly invites inner sweetness: speech, mood and intention turning palatable to others. Contemplate offering her a spoon of honey in the heart, then practise one honeyed act of speech today, letting kindly tone carry firm truth without flattery.  Comparable Vedic esteem for honeyed oblations confirms that nature's sweetness, rightly offered, becomes a vehicle of communion rather than indulgence.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-105"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-511", title: "Bandhinyādisamanvitā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is accompanied by bandhini and other shaktis. Bandhinī and her five companions attend Kākinī, one presiding over each petal of the six-petalled lotus, with allied forms named in wider lore as protectors of the waters. Sam-anvitā means closely attended, picturing the centre as a court rather than a solitary point. No faculty works alone: each sense-current has its guardian intelligence keeping rhythm and boundary. This consoles the seeker overwhelmed by unruly feeling, since help is already stationed at every gate. Contemplate the six petals as six small lamps with watchful keepers, breathing evenly while entrusting each emotional surge to its appointed guardian.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-105"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-512", title: "Dadhyannāsaktahṛdayā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is particularly fond of offerings made with curd. Dadhi means curd or yoghurt, anna means rice, āsakta-hṛdayā means a heart tenderly attached, showing Kākinī's special love for cooling curd-rice. After honey's sweetness comes this soothing, cultured food that calms heat and settles digestion, mirroring how devotion should cool the fever of craving. Great powers are pleased by simple domestic dishes, teaching that sincerity flavours the offering more than rarity. Contemplate preparing or eating plain food as though placing it in her hands, and cultivate a cooling presence that soothes agitated company without suppressing lively joy.  Her cooling diet symbolizes emotional tempering, showing how simple wholesome habits quietly prepare the nervous system for deeper illumination.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-105"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-513", title: "Kākinīrūpadhāriṇī", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is in the form of kākinī yoginī. Rūpa-dhāriṇī means wearing this form as her working vesture, closing the ten-name arc that began at the six-petalled lotus. All preceding details of faces, weapons, colour, pride, tissue, foods and attendants are now gathered and sealed as one identity. The repetition teaches a method: study the parts, then unite them in a single living presence rather than leaving analysis scattered. Doctrine and devotion meet in recognition. Contemplate Kākinī whole for a few breaths, letting individual attributes dissolve into maternal attendance, and dedicate the resulting composure to Lalitā as the source of every yoginī face.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-105"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-514", title: "Mūlādhārāmbujārūḍhā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is resident in the lotus in the mūlādhāra. Mūla means root, ādhāra means support, ambuja means lotus, picturing Sākinī enthroned on the four-petalled red lotus at the perineum. Its petals bear four consonants with bindus, its square pericarp bristles with spears, and within shine the earth seed laṁ, a flashing downward triangle, the desire seed klīṁ, and the coiled serpent power around the inner emblem, whose mouth normally seals the central passage. Here sleeps the life energy that Lalitā herself inhabits most subtly. Contemplate the root as firm ground rather than force, breathing into the pelvic floor with patience and ethical steadiness so awakening, when ripe, rises safely.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-106"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-515", title: "Pañcavaktrā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who has five faces. Pañca means five, vaktra means face, marking Sākinī as regent of the fifth station in this hymn's reckoning. Her five faces mirror the five great elements of space, air, fire, water and earth, showing the root centre already contains the whole material alphabet in seed. Lore of a five-faced mystic diagram gifted for arduous penance underlines how concentrated resolve opens many-sided vision. Multiplicity at the base promises integration above. Contemplate the five elements settling in the body from crown space down to firm earth, breathing gratitude for each, until solidity feels transparent to awareness.  Elemental faces further teach respect for matter, inviting ecological reverence where earth, water, fire, air and space are all her vestures.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-106"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-516", title: "Asthisaṃsthitā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who resides in the bones. Asthi means bone, saṁsthitā means firmly abiding, assigning Sākinī the fifth tissue layer counted inward from skin. Bones give posture, endurance and marrow shelter, so her presence there signifies hidden support beneath all activity. When the hymn calls this the fifth station, anatomy and cosmology confirm each other: structure below upholds spirit above. Remembering her in the skeleton steadies practice during fatigue or fear. Contemplate the spine and limbs as a quiet temple framework upheld by her, standing or sitting erect yet relaxed, and feel effort anchored in something older and calmer than passing moods.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-106"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-517", title: "Aṅkuśādipraharaṇā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who holds the goad and other weapons. Aṅkuśa, the elephant-hook, leads the list, joined in some accounts by lotus, book and the gesture of wisdom, and in sterner ascetic lists by trident, staff, water vessel and rosary. The divergence between manuals is frankly noted, reminding seekers not to idolize iconographic detail but to grasp function: guidance, purity, learning and renunciation. At the root, discipline must be kind yet firm, like training a strong animal. Contemplate the hook as a gentle inner correction that returns attention without violence, pairing each correction with study, simplicity and remembrance of the Mother.  Renunciant emblems among her gear commend simplicity of needs, showing that lighter baggage steadies the climb of attention.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-106"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-518", title: "Varadādiniṣevitā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is attended by varadā and other shaktis. Varadā, whose name means giver of boons, leads three companions associated with prosperity, peace and learning, all serving Sākinī at the root lotus. Niṣevitā means reverently waited upon, picturing even the base centre as royalty with ministers. Spiritual foundations are therefore not barren: generosity, well-being and wisdom already surround the beginner. This encourages steady elementary practice without envy of higher experiences. Contemplate the root court as friends bringing gifts of courage, sufficiency and curiosity, and begin meditation by silently requesting only what helps all beings.  Prosperity, harmony and learning encircling the root promise that foundational virtue naturally attracts the resources needed for further growth.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-106"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-519", title: "Mudgaudanāsaktacittā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is particularly fond of food offerings made of mudga, a lentil. Mudga means green gram, odana means cooked rice, āsakta-citta means a mind lovingly inclined, describing Sākinī's delight in this nourishing mixture prepared with sweetener, coconut, spice, ghee and milk by careful method. Such sattvic food builds clean strength for holding intense energy, linking diet with spiritual capacity. The Goddess honours the humble lentil, showing no nourishment is too plain for sanctification. Contemplate cooking and eating as quiet ritual, choosing fresh moderate food, preparing it attentively, and offering the first thought of the meal to the Mother at the root.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-107"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-520", title: "Sākinyambāsvarūpiṇī", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is in the form of sākinī yoginī. As with earlier centres, the formula of mother plus yoginī gathers the preceding seven details into one maternal identity presiding over the root lotus. Sākinī is not a lesser delegate but Lalitā concentrated at the foundation, guarding bone, bearing kindly weapons, waited on by givers of good. Closing the group this way teaches completion: fully recognize one station before straining toward the next. Contemplate the root mother briefly as the foundation stone of a temple, feeling the whole inner building rest securely upon her, and resolve to keep basic vows of honesty, kindness and moderation.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-107"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-521", title: "Ājñācakrābjanilayā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who resides in the two-petaled lotus in the Ājnācakra. Ājñā means command, locating Hākinī between the eyebrows on a white two-petalled lotus inscribed with the seeds haṁ and kṣaṁ. She is envisioned fair with six faces and six arms bearing gestures of assurance and gifts of knowledge, seated where mind in its subtlest form dwells and the syllable Oṁ is enthroned. Here sun, moon and fire currents meet, half-moon turning to steady flame as realization ripens, and the teacher's guidance is inwardly heard. Contemplate a calm luminous point between the brows, softening the forehead and eyes, and listen for conscience as the Guru's quiet command.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-107"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-522", title: "Śuklavarṇā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is white in color. Śukla means bright white, the complexion of Hākinī reflecting the clarified purity of the brow centre. Below, colours were red, golden and dark; here opacity yields to translucence as thought approaches its source. Whiteness signals sattva triumphant, motives grown honest and attention undivided. Yet white is also beginning, like blank paper awaiting true writing. Contemplate clear moon-white light between the eyebrows washing mental images clean, and practise truthfulness in one small matter today so outer conduct matches inner brightness.  Lunar clarity at this height invites transparent motives, where hidden agendas dissolve under cool and honest self-scrutiny.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-107"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-523", title: "Ṣaḍānanā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who has six faces. Ṣaṭ means six, ānana means face, counting Hākinī's faces as the five elements plus mind itself, fitting her rank as sixth station and controller of the five lower centres. Each lower power reports upward, while mind integrates their subtle essences of sound, touch, sight, taste and smell into coherent command. Her many faces therefore mean synthesis, not confusion. Contemplate the senses gathering like counsellors before a calm chairperson at the brow, noticing sounds, sensations and thoughts without obeying each immediately, then choosing one wise action aligned with deeper purpose.  Mind ruling elements portrays mature self-governance, where witnessing awareness directs sensory energies instead of being dragged by them.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-107"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-524", title: "Majjāsaṃsthā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is the presiding deity of the bone marrow. Majjā means marrow hidden within bone, saṁsthā means indwelling, placing Hākinī in the sixth and most interior tissue layer. Marrow silently generates the blood that sustains life, just as the brow centre silently generates the resolve that sustains practice. The hymn's order thus joins subtle anatomy with spiritual ascent: deeper tissue, higher centre, finer responsibility. Contemplate vitality welling from inner depths rather than forced from outside, resting attention at the brow while sensing quiet strength in the bones, and conserve energy through moderation in speech and excitement.  Interior marrow symbolism points to hidden reserves of resolve, drawn upon in adversity when surface enthusiasm runs dry.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-108"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-525", title: "Haṃsavatīmukhyaśaktisamanvitā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is accompanied by the shaktis hamsavatī and kṣamāvati (in the two petals of the lotus). Haṁsavatī and Kṣamāvatī embody the two brow-petalled seeds haṁ and kṣaṁ, their names beginning with their petal letters according to the system's elegant correspondence. Mukhya means chief, showing how mantra sound, letter, colour, face and attendant all cohere in one science of resonance. Nothing here is arbitrary ornament. Contemplate softly intoning Oṁ and feeling two gentle wings of attention balance at the brows, then study sacred syllables with a teacher's guidance rather than mechanical repetition, honouring sound as conscious power.  Twin seed-sounds guarding two petals show how sacred phonemes structure consciousness, inviting study of sound under qualified guidance.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-108"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-526", title: "Haridrānnaikarasikā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is fond of food seasoned with turmeric. Haridrā means turmeric, anna means rice, eka-rasikā means devoted connoisseur, giving Hākinī a bright golden dish matching her white radiance. Turmeric heals, purifies and colours, an apt emblem for the brow where discernment should tinge all experience with health. Even at this lofty station the Mother accepts homely seasoning, proving sublimity does not despise simplicity. Contemplate letting a warm golden tint of goodwill colour every perception today, especially toward difficult people, asking that judgment be both clear and kind.  Turmeric's healing gold emblemizes discerning perception that both illuminates and disinfects, keeping judgment benevolent yet penetrating.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-108"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-527", title: "Hākinīrūpadhāriṇī", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is in the form of hākinī devī. This sealing name gathers the preceding six brow-centre details into the single maternal figure of Hākinī. Rūpa-dhāriṇī again means consciously wearing this guise, reminding seekers that concentration at one point invokes the undivided Goddess. The command wheel is thus both destination of lower ascent and doorway to the crown beyond. Pause here rather than rushing upward. Contemplate Hākinī as a serene gatekeeper between effort and grace, surrendering personal will with the prayer, let thy command, not mine, prevail in thought, word and deed.  Gatekeeping at the brow counsels pausing between inspiration and action, submitting impulses to conscience before they harden into deeds.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-108"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-528", title: "Sahasradalapadmasthā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who resides in the thousand-petaled lotus. Sahasra-dala means thousand-petalled, naming Yākinī enthroned at the crown beyond the six centres, reached through the tiny aperture of union at the head's summit. Its pericarp outshines the full moon with rainbow rays recapitulating lower colours from red to violet, while its central point of emptiness, fine beyond measure, hosts the blissful meeting of Śiva and Śakti and the release of immortal sweetness. Here effort surrenders into grace and doership dissolves. Contemplate the crown as open sky rather than a place to grasp, resting in silent availability and dedicating all practice to the welfare of beings.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-109"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-529", title: "Sarvavarṇopaśobhitā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is radiant in many colors. Sarva-varṇa means every colour and every letter, for varṇa in Sanskrit holds both senses, so Yākinī shines with the full spectrum and the full alphabet repeating across her thousand petals. One reading sees letters cycling forward and backward endlessly, picturing inexhaustible expressive power gathered into silence. All speech originates here and returns here. Contemplate the crown as white light containing all colours, breathing quietly while sensing every language, art and thought as her shimmer, and use words today as though borrowing jewels to be returned polished.  Alphabet and rainbow together declare expressive plenitude, where every tongue and talent finds its source in her silent shining.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-109"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-530", title: "Sarvāyudhadharā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who holds all the known weapons. Sarva-āyudha means every weapon, fitting Yākinī of the infinite crown where all powers converge beyond reckoning. Ancient Vedic praise of thousandfold missiles in divine arms is invoked to suggest plenitude no list can exhaust, and each practitioner's experience here differs. Total weaponry means total protection: no adversity lacks its corresponding grace. Contemplate fear by placing it before this all-equipped Mother, asking not for escape but for the fitting strength, whether endurance, insight, help or release, and trust her armoury wider than imagination.  Limitless armoury converts dread into reliance, since no conceivable trial falls outside the jurisdiction of her inventive protection.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-109"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-531", title: "Śuklasaṃsthitā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who resides in the semen. Śukla here names the subtlest generative essence, semen and ova, in which Yākinī dwells at the crown. The paradox is deliberate: highest transcendence governs most intimate generativity, creation of bodies below mirroring dissolution of mental constructs above. Her office links continence, creativity and compassion. Honoured, this essence becomes luminous vitality; squandered, attention scatters. Contemplate the crown's white radiance blessing the whole body temple, resolving to channel creative energy into study, service and devotion rather than compulsive indulgence, so vitality ascends as clarity.  Generative essence throned highest hallows fertility in all senses, calling for chaste stewardship of creative power in thought and body.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-109"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-532", title: "Sarvatomukhī", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who has faces turned in all directions. Sarvatas means everywhere, mukha means face, ascribing to Yākinī infinite faces beholding every quarter. Scriptural visions of the cosmic person with eyes, hands and feet on all sides, blazing like a thousand simultaneous suns, give this image its metaphysical weight: at the crown, awareness is no longer directional. Nothing is behind her back. Contemplate being completely seen and utterly safe at once, letting hidden shame and secret pride surface before impartial radiance, and practise seeing every person encountered today as facing the same all-faced Mother.  All-directional faces abolish hiding, inviting radical honesty that confesses faults and consecrates strengths before her total regard.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-109"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-533", title: "Sarvaudanaprītacittā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is pleased by all offerings of food. Sarva-odana means every kind of cooked food, prīta-citta means a heart gladdened, for Yākinī at the infinite crown transcends preference. Earlier yoginīs loved jaggery rice, honey, curd rice, saffron rice and green gram; here all gifts are welcome because infinitude excludes nothing pure. This crowns the food sequence with radical hospitality. Contemplate broadening acceptance in daily life, receiving praise and blame, plenty and lack, as materials for thanksgiving, and share food without ranking giver or dish, seeing the Mother taste through every mouth.  Universal hospitality at the crown heals picky sectarianism, teaching the heart to bless unfamiliar customs and unexpected guests alike.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-110"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-534", title: "Yākinyambāsvarūpiṇī", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is in the form of the yākinī yoginī. Mother Yākinī seals the seven crown names and with them the entire procession of presiding yoginīs from root to summit. Ambā again insists this awesome infinitude is maternal, and svarūpiṇī declares it Lalitā's own form, not another's. The long pilgrimage through tissues, tastes, colours and courts ends where it began: in the Mother. Contemplate the whole spinal path as her single body, breathing from base to crown with devotion, then rest in silence, allowing analysis to melt into presence and presence into wordless gratitude.  Final maternal sealing gathers the long ascent into rest, where mapping yields to loving and loving yields to wordless union.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-110"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-535", title: "Svāhā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the object of the invocation 'svāhā' at the end of mantras chanted while offering oblations to the fire in yāga ceremonies. Svāhā seals oblations to gods, and the seers identify her with the fiery consort power and the mother of divine visage. After the yoginī circuit, attention returns from inner centres to the rite itself: Lalitā is both recipient and utterance, fire and offering word. Every dedicated act, poured with the inward exclamation of surrender, reaches her. Contemplate daily work as oblation, mentally uttering svāhā while giving time, food or care, so routine action becomes sacrificial flame.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-110"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-536", title: "Svadhā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the object of the 'svadhā' invocation at the end of mantras. Where svāhā feeds the gods, svadhā nourishes departed ancestors, and lore names the two as twin powers of sacred fire. A famous allegory pictures the household life itself as a holy cow whose teats are these ritual exclamations, teaching that family duty sustained with reverence maintains gods, seers, ancestors, fellow humans and animals together. Lalitā thus hallows memory and gratitude. Contemplate ancestors with respect before meals or important decisions, offering a silent svadhā of thanks, and undertake one concrete duty toward elders or the departed, such as charity in their name.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-110"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-537", title: "Amatiḥ", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is in the form of ignorance or nescience. Amati names intellect before manifestation, the unawakened mind-stuff poised in perfect equipoise of the three qualities within primordial nature. Before creation stirs, consciousness lies unmanifest and undifferentiated, and she is that too, not only wisdom but its seed-dark ground. This sobering identity prevents contempt for confusion: even nescience is her veiled face. Contemplate moments of dullness or indecision without panic, recognizing unmanifest potential awaiting balance to tip toward knowing, and patiently invoke light through study, prayer and honest self-observation.  Unmanifest darkness as her face forbids despair in confusion, since night itself belongs to the Mother awaiting dawn within.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-110"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-538", title: "Medhā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is in the form of wisdom (knowledge). Medhā is individual intelligence ripened from the dark equipoise of amati into discriminating light, proving her presence across the whole arc from unmanifest to manifest. Sacred hymns invoke her as the all-penetrating goddess of intellect who arrives with nourishment, swiftness and seers' praise, and wisdom is prized as indispensable for recognizing the Self. Paired names thus teach evolution of mind under her gaze. Contemplate intelligence as gift held in trust, dedicating study sessions to her with the resolve, may this understanding serve truth, and test every insight by whether it increases humility and kindness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-110"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-539", title: "Śrutiḥ", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is in the form of the vedas. Śruti means what is heard, the revealed Vedas received by seers, and she is their very substance. Sound as ultimate reality takes verbal body in hymn, chant and formula, so approaching scripture attentively approaches her. This does not confine her to books, since all four Vedas together still only gesture toward her living fullness. Contemplate daily recitation or listening as audience with the Mother of sound, pronouncing clearly, pausing gratefully, and translating one heard truth into conduct, so hearing ripens into embodiment.  Audible revelation as her body elevates listening into worship, where careful attention to chant reshapes the listener inwardly.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-110"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-540", title: "Smṛtiḥ", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is in the form of smṛti (works based on the meaning of vedas). Smṛti means remembered tradition flowing from revelation, making difficult Vedic insight intelligible through law, story, philosophy and recollection. She is also memory itself, the cohesive power praised as divine presence in human thought. Without faithful remembrance, hearing evaporates; with it, wisdom guides choice. Contemplate study followed by deliberate recall, reviewing one teaching each evening and asking how it interprets today's events, thus weaving revelation and experience into a single fabric of mindful living.  Living memory as her form guards continuity, linking ancestral wisdom with present inquiry so novelty remains rooted and rootedness stays alive.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-110"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-541", title: "Anuttamā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the best; She who is not excelled by anyone. An-uttamā negates any superior, affirming her as the unsurpassed reality celebrated in teachings that none equal or exceed the supreme. The same word hints at intellect needing no derivation from another, since she is self-luminous understanding. Supremacy here means refuge: whatever power troubles the heart, a greater gentleness already surrounds it. Contemplate hierarchy worries dissolving before her peerlessness, silently affirming, no fear is higher than the Mother, and act from that assurance with quiet courage tempered by reverence for all beings.  Peerless supremacy steadies comparison-sick hearts, replacing rivalry with refuge in one who outshines every competitor through love.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-110"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-542", title: "Puṇyakīrtiḥ", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She whose fame is sacred or righteous. Puṇya means merit and sanctity, kīrti means renown, so her very reputation purifies; merely thinking of her cleanses the mind and she shares fair fame with devotees. Parallel divine-name lore likewise praises the holy as washer of faults. Glory here is medicinal rather than ornamental. Contemplate reputation differently, seeking to be known only for reliability and kindness, and praise one overlooked good deed in another person today, letting celebration of virtue spread her sanctifying fame.  Sanctifying renown turns praise into purification, so celebrating goodness in others becomes a discreet form of self-cleansing and quiet prayer.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-111"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-543", title: "Puṇyalabhyā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is attained only by righteous souls. Puṇya-labhyā means reachable through merit, for accumulated goodness and scriptural grounding prepare the vessel to perceive her. Tradition warns that craving alone cannot see her, since desire projects its own image, while a celebrated hymn marvels that even high gods worship her and only prior virtue enables praise or prostration. Grace crowns effort without being bought by it. Contemplate one small virtuous discipline undertaken without display, whether truthfulness, charity or restraint, and offer its fruit to her, trusting steady goodness to thin the veil.  Merit-gated access dignifies ethics, presenting honesty, generosity and restraint as the actual keys turning her otherwise hidden lock.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-111"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-544", title: "Puṇyaśravaṇakīrtanā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who bestows merit on anyone who hears of Her and praises Her. Puṇya-śravaṇa-kīrtanā joins hearing and singing her glories as themselves meritorious, so reciting this thousand-name hymn or listening with faith already participates in grace. Closing traditions of both Viṣṇu and Lalitā litanies agree that remembrance sanctifies speaker and hearer alike. Devotion is thus accessible without elaborate qualification. Contemplate joining or hosting a brief group recitation, listening as attentively as chanting, and notice how shared sacred sound steadies restless hearts into common peace.  Audible devotion democratizes holiness, placing liberation within reach of all who will sincerely listen, repeat and remember.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-111"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-545", title: "Pulomajārcitā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is worshipped by pulomaja (Indra's wife). Pulomajā, daughter of Puloman and queen of the gods, worshipped Lalitā in desperate straits when her husband lost throne and honour through wrongdoing and hiding. Advised by preservation itself, gods and sages turned to the supreme Mother, whose grace restored order and protected fidelity. The tale honours moral repair over mere power: even celestial pride must seek the Goddess through humility. Contemplate appealing to her when entangled by consequences of one's own mistakes, combining honest confession, patient worship and practical amends rather than evasion.  Royal repentance through her worship proves that no downfall lies beyond remedial grace when pride surrenders into supplication.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-111"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-546", title: "Bandhamocanī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is free from bonds; She who gives release from bondage. Bandha means bondage woven by nescience into craving and clinging, mocanī means she who frees. She who is ever unbound liberates surrendered souls, loosening identification with restless wanting and inaugurating movement toward freedom. Liberation begins not with escape from circumstance but release of grasping within it. Contemplate one current attachment by naming it plainly, breathing space around it, and praying to be shown right use or release, then practise a small renunciation that proves inner freedom.  Untying of inner knots begins with confession of clinging, followed by gradual retraining of desire toward liberality and peace.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-111"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-547", title: "Barbarālakā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who has wavy locks of hair;. Barbarā or bandhura curls ripple across her forehead like gentle waves, a tender human detail amid cosmic majesties. Variant readings preserve the same affectionate image, showing tradition savouring her beauty rather than disputing syllables. Wavy hair suggests playful movement within perfect order, liveliness that never breaks grace. Contemplate her gentle loveliness when abstraction grows dry, picturing moon-soft curls stirring with compassion, and let an answering playfulness lighten austerity, keeping discipline supple with joy.  Playful curls soften metaphysical grandeur, keeping devotion tender and approachable amid formidable cosmic architectures. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-111"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-548", title: "Vimarśarūpiṇī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is in the form of vimarsha (reflection or meaning). Vimarśa means self-aware reflection, the active knowing power complementing prakāśa, self-luminous stillness. Without reflection, light would sleep unwitnessed; without light, reflection would have nothing to mirror. As Śiva's dynamic counterpart, she executes what stillness intends, administering creation through fivefold acts while remaining one with the inactive ground. Soul mirrors this union, helpless alone but potent with nature. Contemplate awareness noticing itself noticing, resting as the witness who also cares, and dedicate one deliberate action today to embodying silent understanding in helpful form.  Mirroring light and awareness resolves false rivalry between stillness and action, revealing agency as luminosity in motion.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-112"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-549", title: "Vidyā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is in the form of knowledge. Vidyā means liberating discernment that deliberates truly and frees, the sequel to creative reflection. Having displayed vast powers, the hymn now names their crown: pure wisdom whose dawn grants mastery over all subsidiary energies and turns the knower toward primordial light until knower and known unite. Lower learnings inform, but this supreme knowing transforms. Contemplate study as pilgrimage rather than accumulation, pausing after reading to ask, how does this dissolve one knot of ego, and pray for the higher learning that makes conduct luminous.  Transformative knowing surpasses information, demanding that each learned truth visibly loosen some chain of selfish habit.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-112"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-550", title: "Viyadādi jagatprasūḥ", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the Mother of the universe, which is the aggregate of all the elements starting with the ether. Viyat-ādi starts with space, then air, fire, water and earth unfold in succession, reversing at dissolution, as a celebrated Upanishadic genealogy teaches. Prasū means birth-giver, identifying her with the Self from which spaciousness first emerges. Cosmos is thus her family history. Contemplate vastness condensing into form during inhalation and form dissolving into vastness during exhalation, honouring her as both cradle and rest, and treat material things as borrowed maternity ward gifts.  Elemental motherhood fosters cosmic kinship, where sky and soil are siblings and exploitation yields to grateful caretaking.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-112"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-551", title: "Sarvavyādhipraśamanī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who removes all diseases and sorrows. Sarva-vyādhi covers disorders of body and mind together, praśamanī means she who thoroughly calms them. Sound mind needs a capable body to bear intense energy, while bodily health without clarity remains fragile, so her healing is integral. As herself ever free of ailment, she communicates wholeness rather than merely removing symptoms. Contemplate health as stewardship for realization, maintaining sleep, diet, exercise and emotional honesty, and when ill, pray for both cure and the wisdom suffering can teach, using recovery as renewed offering.  Integral healing joins physician, priest and friend, treating symptom, soul and circumstance as one field of her compassion.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-112"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-552", title: "Sarvamṛtyunivāriṇī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who guards Her devotees from all. Mṛtyu means death in all its forms, from accident and illness to untimely loss, nivāriṇī means she who wards off. Deeper teaching hears here the promise of release from repeated birth: knowing oneness with the deathless frees from death's shackles, and final death becomes merger rather than mere ending. Fuelled by stored action, bodies run their course, but she can exhaust the fuel into freedom. Contemplate mortality as tutor, living each day as preparation for a conscious farewell, and practise small dyings of resentment and vanity so the great transition finds the heart rehearsed in letting go.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-112"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-553", title: "Agragaṇyā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is to be considered the foremost. Agra means front or first, gaṇyā means to be counted, hailing her as eldest in creation and foremost in worship. Though the still ground has no parent, she emerges as its kinetic power to project and sustain worlds, present alone with him before beginnings and after endings, across shifting ages. Primacy means precedence in honour and resort. Contemplate placing her first in the day's agenda, offering morning's initial attention before news or worry, and consult conscience before expedience when decisions compete, letting the foremost claim shape lesser rankings.  Primordial precedence reorders ambitions, placing devotion before acquisition so lesser goods find their proper subordinate places.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-113"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-554", title: "Acintyarūpā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is of a form beyond the reach of thought. Acintya means unthinkable, rūpā means formed, paradoxically forming the formless to hint at boundless omnipresence beyond mind and qualities. Earlier negations of comprehensibility return here as affirmation: amorphous, all-pervading, yet mothering souls and nature into creative union symbolized in mystic emblems. Thought fails not from defect but from excess of light. Contemplate intellectual humility by pausing concepts and resting in open wonder, admitting, this exceeds me, then serve the mystery concretely through one kind act that needs no theory.  Unthinkable form protects mystery from mental idolatry, keeping concepts as fingers pointing toward her moonlit boundlessness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-113"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-555", title: "Kalikalmaṣanāśinī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the destroyer of the sins of the age of kali. Kali names the present age of brevity and confusion, kalmaṣa means stain, nāśinī means destroyer, promising her special accessibility when darkness thickens. Vast traditional time-cycles frame our moment as a small but potent window where sincere remembrance carries unusual weight. She meets collective decline not with despair but with fierce maternal cleansing. Contemplate personal and social faults without cynicism, choosing one corrective habit such as honest speech, restraint in consumption or regularity in prayer, trusting small fidelity amid general forgetfulness to invoke her stain-removing grace.  Stain-destroying in dark ages offers hope proportionate to gloom, assuring that sincerity shines brightest precisely when surroundings dim.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-113"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-556", title: "Kātyāyanī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the daughter of a sage named kata. Kata's lineage yields Kātyāyanī, also hailed as the united radiance of all divinities and regent of a mystic sanctuary at the brow, networked with other sacred seats across the subtle body. Etymology plays on resting upon the head or cosmic ground, suggesting she crowns both cosmos and seeker. Many splendours converging into one daughter teaches unity in diversity. Contemplate gathering fragmented devotions to various ideals into single-hearted attention on her, and honour teachers of different temperaments as rays of one maternal brightness.  Convergent radiance in one daughter reconciles many altars, letting diverse devotions mature into single-hearted adoration.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-113"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-557", title: "Kālahantrī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the destroyer of time (death). Kāla means time and its terminus death, hantrī means slayer, declaring her terminus of termination for those awake to the Self. As creatrix of time she is not its victim; souls bound by scheduled action meet their hour, while knowers transcend schedule into timeless presence. Doubled names on conquering death stress that liberation, not longevity, is the point. Contemplate time as her leash rather than master, using calendars and deadlines as reminders of precious opportunity, and redeem one wasted hour daily for remembrance that outlasts hours.  Time-slaying reveals eternity within temporality, inviting practitioners to spend finite hours on infinite remembrance.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-113"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-558", title: "Kamalākṣaniṣevitā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She in whom viṣṇu takes refuge. Kamala-akṣa means lotus-eyed, naming the preserver who worships her and, lore says, received power to assume an enchanting feminine guise for restoring cosmic balance. A celebrated hymn confirms this adoration, subtly honouring womanhood as sacred capacity rather than mere charm. Even preservation seeks the Mother's sanction. Contemplate mutual reverence among different paths, honouring sustaining virtues of patience and care in oneself and others, and support someone guarding goodness under difficulty, recognizing their service as worship of her.  Lotus-eyed adoration crowns preservation with devotion, showing that maintaining goodness itself depends on contemplating her. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-113"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-559", title: "Tāmbūlapūritamukhī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She whose mouth is full from chewing betel. Tāmbūla means betel leaf with aromatic camphor blend, pūrita-mukhī means mouth delightfully filled, picturing her lips reddened and fragrant from gracious enjoyment. The image continues earlier hints of her savouring consecrated flavours, divinity tasting human hospitality with visible pleasure. Sacred sensuality here sanctifies embodiment instead of shaming delight. Contemplate offering beauty and fragrance to the senses in moderation as worship, savouring food, scent or music gratefully while dedicating enjoyment upward, so pleasure becomes praise rather than bondage.  Fragrant enjoyment sanctifies taste and breath, teaching that consecrated pleasure can carry prayer as surely as austerity.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-114"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-560", title: "Dāḍimīkusumaprabhā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who shines like a pomegranate flower. Dāḍimī-kusuma means pomegranate blossom, prabhā means radiance, comparing her glow to deep red petals favoured for worship alongside hibiscus. Poet-saints liken her complexion to this blossom, and the hymn's repeated redness encodes compassion made visible, vitality wedded to tenderness. Colour becomes catechism. Contemplate her ruby-pomegranate glow in the heart when feeling grey, breathing warmth into cold judgments, and place a red flower or kindly word before her image as token of rekindled affection.  Pomegranate splendour keeps compassion visibly warm, guarding philosophy from chilling into abstraction divorced from tenderness. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-114"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-561", title: "Mṛgākṣī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She whose eyes are long and beautiful like those of a doe. Mṛga-akṣī means deer-eyed, borrowing the gentlest, most alert gaze in nature to suggest the indescribable. Deer glance swiftly in all directions, and she as cosmic administrator likewise oversees everything while singling out ripe devotees for liberating glances that brook no delay. Parallel litanies praise the same fawn-like regard. Contemplate her compassionate side-glance resting on you during distraction or shame, straightening without startling, and imitate it by noticing one overlooked person today with timely encouraging attention.  Deer-like vigilance blends alertness with gentleness, watching all needs keenly while approaching wounded hearts without startling them.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-114"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-562", title: "Mohinī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is enchanting. Mohinī means the bewitching one, the seers' awed confession when beauty outruns vocabulary. Her fascination is compounded of wisdom, capability and tenderness, not mere appearance, and lore links the preserver's captivating guise to worship of her. Holy enchantment awakens rather than entraps. Contemplate beauty as summons to goodness, pausing before anything lovely to ask what virtue it mirrors, and cultivate inner attractiveness through clarity, competence and compassion that draw others toward truth without manipulation.  Holy enchantment reforms aesthetics, judging beauty by its power to awaken wisdom, capability and kindness in beholders. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-114"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-563", title: "Mukhyā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the first. Mukhya means foremost, primary and authoritative among divinities, echoing scriptural boasts of being born before gods and worlds. She is not only first in sequence but principal in significance and participant in all subsequent creation. Primacy invites priority. Contemplate giving her the first fruits of energy, wealth and thought rather than leftovers, beginning enterprises with brief invocation, and test choices by whether they honour what is truly primary over what merely clamours.  Foundational primacy simplifies decisions, measuring options by nearness to the ultimate rather than loudness of immediate appeal. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-114"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-564", title: "Mṛḍānī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the wife of mṛḍa (shiva). Mṛḍa names the gracious auspicious one, and Mṛḍānī is his consort, giver of the happiness flowing from reflective awareness. Stillness weds activity, benediction weds responsiveness, and their union yields welfare. Marriage imagery encodes metaphysics of inseparable calm and care. Contemplate relationships as mirrors of this union, balancing receptivity with initiative, silence with speech, and offer one act that brings happiness specifically through understanding rather than possession.  Auspicious union balances quietude and responsiveness, modelling partnerships where calm strength weds attentive care. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-114"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-565", title: "Mitrarūpiṇī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the friend of everyone (universe). Mitra means friend and also the sun, sustainer of all life, whose form she wears. Mystic worship maps fire, moon and sun to stillness, power and their union, placing her as approachable solar mediation. Friendship here means life-giving accessibility without favouritism. Contemplate the sun as her friendly eye, receiving its light impartially, and practise universal friendliness by wishing well even toward competitors, letting warmth coexist with principled firmness.  Solar friendship universalizes benevolence, shining impartially while respecting each creature's distinct season and soil. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-114"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-566", title: "Nityatṛptā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is eternally contented. Nitya-tṛptā means perpetually satisfied, having no lack because infinitude cannot want. Sayings on the infinite remaining full though fullness flows from fullness illuminate this poise. A companion name praises contentment even with perishable gifts, suggesting she equally savours the imperishable offering of the Self. Contemplate sufficiency amid craving by pausing before next acquisition to affirm present gifts, and practise contentment as generosity, sharing from fullness rather than hoarding from fear.  Infinite satisfaction overflows into sharing, turning sufficiency from private comfort into public generosity. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-115"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-567", title: "Bhaktanidhiḥ", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the treasure of the devotees. Bhakta-nidhi means treasury for the loving, the inexhaustible store she opens whenever sincerely sought, celebrated again near the hymn's close. Devotion is capital that never devalues, and she is both banker and bullion. Need discovers abundance. Contemplate spiritual poverty honestly, listing inner lacks without shame, then petition her treasury specifically for one virtue, resolving to spend it promptly in service so the deposit multiplies through use.  Devotee treasury personalizes providence, assuring each supplicant that heaven keeps a labelled account of needed graces. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-115"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-568", title: "Niyantrī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who controls and guides all beings on the right path. Niyantrī means female guide and governor, administering the universe not as distant mechanism but directed care. Guidance implies both governance and tutoring, steering events while instructing conscience. Trust and effort cooperate. Contemplate her hand on life's reins during confusion, asking less for control of outcomes than clarity of next right step, and accept one unwelcome limit as possible guidance, adjusting course with humble attentiveness.  Guiding governance steadies trust amid volatility, attributing both open doors and closed ones to her tutoring wisdom. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-115"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-569", title: "Nikhileśvarī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the ruler of all. Nikhila means whole or entire, īśvarī means sovereign lady, extending guidance into universal rule as befits the all. Because she alone guides everything, sole sovereignty is not tyranny but coherence. Many wills move within one encompassing will. Contemplate surrendering private absolutism by prefacing plans with openness to revision, and honour legitimate authority in family, work and community as refracted stewardship, obeying conscientiously while reserving ultimate allegiance to her.  Total sovereignty integrates fragmented loyalties, gathering family, craft and creed under one compassionate constitution. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-115"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-570", title: "Maitryādivāsanālabhyā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is to be attained by love and other good dispositions. Maitrī-ādi means friendliness and its companions of compassion, gladness and equanimity taught as stillers of mental turbulence, vāsanā means cultivated inclination, labhyā means attainable. Transforming hatred into love and envy into joy refines the mind into a fit mirror for bliss and eventual merging. Positivity is methodology, not naivety. Contemplate one difficult relationship through the fourfold lens, sending goodwill to the fortunate without jealousy and steadiness toward the harmful without hatred, thus perfuming latent tendencies toward her.  Friendly dispositions as her address teach emotional alchemy, transmuting irritation into goodwill through deliberate practice.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-115"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-571", title: "Mahāpralayasākṣiṇī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is witness to the great dissolution. Mahā-pralaya means total reabsorption when worlds reverse their emergence, element by element into spacious ground, traditionally amidst a cosmic dance of withdrawal. Sākṣiṇī means sole witnessing presence who watches without perishing. A celebrated hymn marvels that while lesser powers dissolve, the divine couple sport on. Contemplate impermanence courageously, viewing endings as rehearsals of this great return, and practise non-clinging by using possessions gratefully while holding identity lightly, anchored in the witness who survives every scene change.  Dissolution witnessing anchors courage amid collapse, remembering one presence abides when every structure returns to silence.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-115"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-572", title: "Parāśaktiḥ", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the original, supreme power. Parā means supreme, śakti means power, naming her consort of the absolute as transcendent yet freely ranging awareness. Lore divides bodily elements between her and the still ground, reserving the tenth and most subtle essence to her, while non-dual Shaiva reflection celebrates her as original universe whose expression is all manifestation and whose womb holds primordial speech. Without her mirroring dynamism, stillness cannot even throb. Contemplate every capacity to know and act as loaned from her, beginning tasks by acknowledging borrowed power and returning credit through humble service.  Supreme dynamism corrects static images of ultimacy, revealing the absolute as vibrantly creative through her ceaseless play.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-116"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-573", title: "Parāniṣṭhā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the supreme end, the supreme abidance. Parā-niṣṭhā means steadfast settlement in the highest, the mind of the mature knower perpetually united with reality. Teachings on knowledge-sacrifice surpassing material sacrifice urge inward turn from ritual paraphernalia toward steady awareness, nourished especially by Upanishadic inquiry. When learning ripens into unshakable experience, she is both path and haven. Contemplate stabilizing one insight into character, choosing a truth already glimpsed and practising it until reflexive, trusting her to convert fleeting understanding into permanent abiding.  Steadfast abiding ripens flashes into character, converting peak insights into plateau virtues through patient repetition. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-116"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-574", title: "Prajñānaghanarupiṇī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is pure, condensed knowledge. Prajñāna-ghana means solidified superior intelligence unpolluted by sensory distortion, the sequel to steadfast abiding. A great Upanishadic image of the Self as seamless pure awareness without inside or outside captures this density, where all dissolves into knowing and realization dawns. Concentration here means homogeneity, not tension. Contemplate simplifying mental diet by reducing noisy inputs and resting in one clear certitude, such as awareness is, allowing scattered cleverness to condense into quiet luminous certainty that informs compassionate action.  Condensed intelligence distils erudition into realization, prizing one lived certitude above libraries of borrowed opinion. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-116"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-575", title: "Mādhvīpānālasā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is languid from drinking wine; She who is not eager for anything. Mādhvī-pāna means draught of honeyed exhilaration, alasā means gracefully languorous, picturing her relaxed in absorptive bliss while contemplating her source. Esoteric reading hears the sweet throat-dripping ambrosia of deep ascent, a spiritual intoxicant of many delights that fixes consciousness in the absolute and renders worldly urgency weightless. She possesses all, hence gives all. Contemplate holy languor by deliberately resting ambition after sincere effort, savouring silent presence without planning, and let bliss soften drivenness into grateful availability.  Blissful languor redeems rest from guilt, showing sabbath-like surrender as fertile ground for creative renewal.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-116"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-576", title: "Mattā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is intoxicated. Mattā means rapturously inebriated, continuing the honeyed languor of absorption, also interpreted as bearing the exhilarated self-sense of the absolute in introverted poise. Paired names together map bliss and its significance: realization may be reached through ascending energy or interior self-inquiry, both culminating in joyful self-forgetfulness. Divine intoxication heals sober ego. Contemplate wholesome self-forgetfulness in chant, service or nature, allowing identity to relax its guard briefly, then return to duties refreshed, carrying a trace of introverted sweetness into extroverted care.  Rapturous absorption refreshes duty, sending practitioners back to tasks carrying fragrance of inward forgetfulness. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-116"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-577", title: "Mātṛkāvarṇarūpiṇī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is in the form of the letters of the alphabet. Mātṛkā-varṇa means the mothering letters, fifty-one seed sounds grouped and worshipped across the six centres in distinct hues, intimately tied to cosmology and the body-temple diagram. Vowels pulse dynamically as power while consonants stabilize as ground, and only their union yields articulate sound, just as stillness and dynamism together yield universe. She is both mother of letters and primal Word. Contemplate speech as sacred energy, pronouncing mantra or kindly words with reverent clarity, and observe how refined sound refines thought and countenance.  Lettered motherhood sacralizes language, charging speakers to utter words as offerings that shape worlds.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-116"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-578", title: "Mahākailāsanilayā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who resides in the great kailāsa. Mahā-kailāsa means the transcendent abode beyond the visible mountain, dwelling of the absolute in utter subtlety, also identified with the pinpoint aperture at the crown where she joins stillness in finest union. Epics affirm her co-residence there as inseparable consort, while mystic stairways of letter, calendar-goddess and hymn-seer meditations culminate toward this height. Home is ultimacy. Contemplate the crown point as boundless mountain peak of silence, lifting aspiration beyond images while honouring images as base camps, and dedicate virtuous acts as provisions for that high ascent.  Peak-dwelling lifts homesickness toward heaven, treating earthly shelters as inns on ascent to boundless domicile.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-117"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-579", title: "Mṛṇālamṛdudorlatā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She whose arms are as soft and cool as the lotus stem. Mṛṇāla means lotus stalk, mṛdu means tender, dorlatā means creeper-like arms, praising her embrace as delicate yet sustaining. Her subtlest serpentine form was earlier likened to lotus fibre, linking power with fragility. Tenderness here is strength refined, able to hold without crushing. Contemplate her gentle arms supporting weary effort, relaxing shoulders and unclenching hands during meditation, and extend that softness outward by handling one fragile person or task today with extra delicacy and patience.  Lotus-soft arms reconcile strength with delicacy, modelling leadership that upholds without bruising fragile growth.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-117"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-580", title: "Mahanīyā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is adorable. Mahanīyā means worthy of greatness and honour, one whom every sincere praise only further befits. The frequent majestic prefix across many names reflects inexhaustible praiseworthiness rather than repetition. Adoration enlarges the adorer. Contemplate praise as practice by enumerating her gifts plainly each morning, from breath to friendship, until gratitude becomes reflexive, and honour someone habitually overlooked with specific appreciative words that dignify both giver and receiver.  Adorable worthiness multiplies praise, for each thanksgiving uncovers further facets of her inexhaustible generosity. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-117"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-581", title: "Dayāmūrtiḥ", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the personification of compassion. Dayā-mūrti means mercy embodied, the predominant quality that opens this thousand-name hymn with motherhood and recurs like a refrain through its length. She sustains even those who never think of her, offering liberation's opportunity to the forgetful as well as the faithful. Compassion is therefore structural, not sentimental. Contemplate receiving unmerited sustenance right now in breath and shelter, letting the heart soften, then transmit one unsolicited kindness to someone who cannot repay, becoming a small local embodiment of her all-embracing mercy.  Embodied mercy mobilizes pity into action, translating tender feeling into concrete relief for tangible suffering.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-117"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-582", title: "Mahāsāmrājyaśālinī", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who controls the great empire of the three worlds. Mahā-sāmrājya means vast dominion over clustered worlds and planets, of which earth is a tiny province, and śālinī means gracefully wielding. Even the transcendent peak falls within her consort-regency, showing tenderness and sovereignty perfectly joined. Empire serves nurture. Contemplate vast scale to relativize anxiety, picturing galaxies turning effortlessly in her palm while she still notices a whispered prayer, then govern your small domain of home and work with calm far-sightedness rather than hurried control.  Graceful empire universalizes responsibility, charging the powerful to govern as trustees of her larger household. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-117"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-583", title: "Ātmavidyā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the knowledge of the self. Ātma-vidyā means science of the Self, also called supreme sacred learning, imparted by her who instructs even the still absolute without attributes. Esoteric identifications link her with profound twilight chants and an eight-syllabled contemplative formula, showing many doors into one self-knowing. To know the knower is her gift. Contemplate turning attention from objects to the aware subject, asking quietly, who hears these thoughts, and rest in the simple sense of being before labels, dedicating that transparency to her.  Self-science interiorizes pilgrimage, turning temples, texts and teachers into mirrors of the aware heart.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-118"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-584", title: "Mahāvidyā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is the seat of exalted knowledge, the knowledge of the self. Mahā-vidyā crowns self-knowledge as greatest among learnings, dissolving distinctions of pleasure and pain into perpetual connected bliss beyond rebirth. Alternative lore hails a protective forest-goddess formula of many seeds as similarly supreme, powerful to dispel hostile forces. Greatness proves itself by freeing. Contemplate equanimity training by meeting one pleasant and one unpleasant event with the same inward salutation, learning to stay tethered to her while sensations pass like weather across an unchanged sky.  Exalted knowing equalizes fortune, meeting honour and insult with the same rooted courtesy. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-118"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-585", title: "Śrīvidyā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is sacred knowledge. Śrī-vidyā names the auspicious fifteen-syllabled mantra science, classed among the most secret paths transcending ritual action, ceremonial procedure and hidden worship toward Self-knowledge. Proper initiation and disciplined recitation under guidance are said to ripen inevitably into freedom. Auspiciousness and efficacy unite. Contemplate approaching mantra with reverent regularity rather than curiosity, maintaining purity of conduct, respectful guidance and steady repetition, so sacred sound gradually re-tunes desire into devotion and devotion into liberating wisdom.  Auspicious mantra-science disciplines longing, channelling desire for wonders into hunger for liberating truth. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-118"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-586", title: "Kāmasevitā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is worshipped by kāmadeva. Kāma-sevitā means served by the lord of love, counted among twelve great framers of her fifteen-syllabled lore, whose tale of fiery dissolution and bodiless persistence teaches that mental worship outlasts physical form. The same root also honours the highest Lord as her devotee, the pair alternately teaching each other mantra mysteries. Love serves wisdom. Contemplate worship beyond bodily display through silent loving attention, offering desires themselves as flowers, and transform one romantic or ambitious longing into fuel for sustained remembrance of her.  Love-served wisdom eroticizes devotion chastely, redirecting passion from possession toward adoring surrender.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-118"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-587", title: "Śrīṣoḍaśākṣarīvidyā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is in the form of the sixteen-syllabled mantra. Ṣoḍaśākṣarī adds the sixteenth lunar digit to the fifteen, unfolding into many seeds whose pañcadaśī core sits framed by mirrored auspicious syllables. Moon-phase symbolism governs its arithmetic, and the personal seed within is entrusted to a discerning teacher, since misplaced power harms. Reserved for those yearning freedom above all, it crowns method with grace. Contemplate the full moon's sixteen digits waxing in the heart as completeness beyond technique, honouring initiation, restraint and longing for liberation over lesser boons.  Sixteen-digit fullness completes method with grace, reminding technicians of transcendence that mercy crowns arithmetic.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-118"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-588", title: "Trikūṭā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is in the three parts. Tri-kūṭā means threefold-peaked, gathering sacred triads of syllable, worlds, functions, awarenesses, qualities, times, mantra divisions and powers into her single lordship over arising, sustaining and dissolving. Seated upon fivefold supports yet ruling every three, she harmonizes multiplicity through rhythm. Threeness becomes a ladder. Contemplate one living triad such as willing, knowing and doing, offering each phase consciously to her, until daily cycles of beginning, continuing and completing feel like quiet participation in her cosmic measure.  Triple peaks rhythm life's cadences, consecrating beginnings, continuings and endings as her triple footfall. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-118"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-589", title: "Kāmakoṭikā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She, of whom kāma (shiva) is a part or an approximate form. Kāma-koṭi joins the beloved still ground with vertical peak or angle, imaging their union both as enthroned pair and as half-and-half single body. Distinguishing attributeless ground from qualified power, then reuniting them, this name locates fulfilment in non-dual embrace rather than solitary ascent. Parallel three-hundred-name lore enshrines the same peak-dwelling. Contemplate union in the heart by sensing still witnessing and lively caring as one movement, letting meditation settle into balanced androgyny of peace and compassion.  Vertical union of beloved and loving dissolves lonely seeking, enthroning companionship at the heart of absoluteness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-118"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-590", title: "Kaṭākṣakiṅkarībhutakamalākoṭisevitā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is attended by millions of lakṣmis who are subdued by Hern mere glances. Kaṭākṣa means side-glance, kiṅkarī-bhūta means rendered servant, kamalā-koṭi means crores of lotus goddesses of fortune, picturing innumerable prosperities lining up at one soft look from her. Where a single goddess's glance enriches a household, her glance commands all enrichment whatsoever. Wealth is thus relativized into attendance. Contemplate prosperity as her handmaiden rather than mistress, requesting only what serves dharma and sharing surplus readily, so money circles back as worship instead of fetter.  Glance-commanded fortunes dethrone mammon, seating generosity as treasurer of all material increase. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-119"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-591", title: "Śiraḥsthitā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who resides in the head. Śiras-sthitā places her at the cranial crown, the sanctum of the human temple where stillness, power and the authentic teacher are together adored. She is herself supreme guide, initiating into sacred formulas and leading beyond outer ritual toward inner realization. Only teachers who turn disciples from performance to self-inquiry deserve installation in this high shrine. Contemplate the crown as classroom of silence, mentally bowing to the lineage of genuine guides, and test teaching by whether it increases humility, clarity and compassion rather than dependence.  Cranial sanctum enthrones authentic guidance, testing every claimant voice by its fruits of freedom and humility.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-119"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-592", title: "Candranibhā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is resplendent like the moon. Candra-nibhā likens her to moonlight glimpsed just below the crown, a classic milestone in ascending meditation signalling ripening calm. Her lunar third of the fifteen-syllabled science cools solar intensity into reflective serenity. Moonlight borrows yet beautifies. Contemplate cool silvery light suffusing the upper head during agitation, breathing slowly while letting thoughts pale like stars at dawn, and cultivate reflective gentleness that mirrors truth without harsh glare.  Moonlike serenity cools fevered ambition, reflecting ideals gently so aspiration enlightens rather than scorches. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-119"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-593", title: "Bhālasthā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who resides in the forehead (between the eyebrows). Bhāla-sthā stations her as the focal point of the seed syllable hrīṁ at the brow, its many sonic components culminating in a luminous dot above the vowel. In the sacred diagram, point and innermost triangle likewise wed still ground with dynamic power. Forehead becomes threshold. Contemplate a fine point of light between the brows gathering scattered attention like a lens gathering sun, holding it softly without strain until thinking clarifies and feeling warms in balanced contemplation.  Brow-point focus gathers vagrancy into vision, lensing diffuse energies into one illuminating beam. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-119"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-594", title: "Indradhanuḥprabhā", category: "Vidyā", summary: "She who is resplendent like the rainbow. Indra-dhanus means rainbow, prabhā means radiance, continuing the brow-point vision with a quarter-sized crescent bow blazing above it in sevenfold colour. This arc prefigures the crown's full spectrum explosion and is contemplated in esoteric adoration of her subtlest diagrammatic form, where point shines enthroned amid prismatic promise. Unity refracts into plenty. Contemplate the brow rainbow when life feels monochrome, recalling one light mothers many hues, and honour diversity of temperaments and gifts as refractions of her single radiance.  Rainbow radiance celebrates pluriform unity, honouring each colour of character as her refracted light. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-119"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-595", title: "Hṛdayasthā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who resides in the heart. Hṛdaya-sthā affirms her dwelling in the cardiac centre, echoing Upanishadic declarations of the thumb-sized luminous presence within. She is to be meditated in the heart lotus, whose core is sometimes called the heart of reality itself, storehouse of universal love where knowers taste lasting happiness. Immanence crowns transcendence. Contemplate the heartbeat as her soft drum, synchronizing breath with quiet remembrance, and practise heartfelt sincerity in one conversation today, letting outer speech match inner feeling as offering to the indweller.  Cardiac indwelling nearest intimacy, making every heartbeat a quiet footstep of the Mother within. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-120"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-596", title: "Raviprakhyā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who shines with the special brilliance of the sun. Ravi-prakhyā means sun-like splendour, locating the solar division of her fifteen-syllabled science in the heart wheel. She blazes in every division, yet each station tints illumination distinctively: here warmth, vigour and revealing clarity. Heart-sun dispels inner chill. Contemplate golden solar radiance expanding from the chest with each breath, energizing resolve while melting resentment, and undertake one courageous generous act that lets inner sunlight become visible service.  Heartfelt sunlight energizes charity, converting warmth of feeling into daylight deeds of courage. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-120"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-597", title: "Trikoṇāntaradīpikā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who shines as a light within the triangle. Tri-koṇa-antara-dīpikā means lamp inside the triangle, kindled at the root's fiery first division. Together with heart-sun and crown-moon she completes a threefold lamp lit across root, heart, brow and crown, sprouting below, developing midway and blossoming near the summit as moving mystic poetry beautifully sings. One flame migrates homeward. Contemplate a small steady flame low in the body lending light upward, grounding lofty aspiration in disciplined vitality, and keep one foundational vow meticulously so higher insight stands on trustworthy fuel.  Triangular lamp grounds sublimity in discipline, feeding lofty flames with oil of humble daily fidelity.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-120"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-598", title: "Dākṣāyaṇī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is satīdevī, the daughter of dakṣa prajāpati. Dākṣāyaṇī recalls her birth as Dakṣa's daughter wed to the great Lord, while the same root means ritual oblations of full and new moon rites in which she inheres as sacrificial presence. Dakṣa further connotes wilful vigour and intellectual strengthening, and his many lunar daughters wed to the moon link discipline with cosmic rhythm. Lineage teaches embodiment. Contemplate honouring both family heritage and willed resolve, observing one regular observance aligned with lunar fortnights, such as fasting, charity or extended prayer, to harmonize personal rhythm with larger order.  Daughterhood of resolve links heritage with will, weaving ancestral gifts into personally chosen vows.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-120"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-599", title: "Daityahantrī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the killer of demons. Daiti-hantrī means slayer of Titanic evil, the demonic qualities of arrogance, cruelty and delusion dramatized in epics. Earlier battle names already celebrated this office, showing protection inseparable from motherhood. Inner demons precede outer ones. Contemplate identifying one persistent unwholesome pattern by name, whether anger, greed or deceit, and invoking her warrior compassion to confront it firmly without self-hatred, replacing each demolition with a constructive virtue installed like a guardian at the cleared gate.  Demon-slaying interiorizes warfare, directing valour first against cruelty, deceit and sloth within. Hold this presence daily with patient affection, returning attention to her whenever it strays into fear, hurry or dullness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-120"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-600", title: "Dakṣayajñavināśinī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the destroyer of the sacrifice conducted by dakṣa. Dakṣa-yajña-vināśinī recalls wreck of rites performed by two proud sacrificers, celestial and human, who slighted the still ground by withholding rightful honour. Righteous fury through its hosts shattered exclusionary ceremony, with her will behind the rectification. Ritual without reverence self-destructs. Contemplate inclusiveness in worship and work, ensuring no worthy participant is slighted through ego or faction, and repair one past exclusion by acknowledgment or restitution, learning that true sacrifice honours all divinity in all beings.  Ruined exclusionary rites warn privileged piety, insisting true sacrifice seats every worthy being at the altar.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-120"] },
];
